<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Gingee Nayaks</h1><p class="st-meta">1509 CE – 1649 CE (approx.) · capital Gingee</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Telugu Nayaka governors of Vijayanagara who ruled northern Tamil country from the near-impregnable fortress of Gingee.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaka</a> governors to administer its Tamil provinces in the early 16th century. Around 1509 one line, traditionally founded by Tubaki Krishnappa Nayaka, was installed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gingee">Gingee</a> (Senji), controlling the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palar-river">Palar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollidam-river">Kollidam</a> rivers, including the coast around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pondicherry">Pondicherry</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gingee">Gingee</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaks</a> strengthened the hill fortress, linking three hills with walls to create one of the strongest forts in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Like the other Nayakas, they collected revenue through local military chiefs and patronised temples such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruvannamalai">Tiruvannamalai</a>. After Vijayanagara's defeat in 1565 they acted increasingly independently, allowing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> to set up a post at Tegnapatnam.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Wars with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/madurai-nayak-dynasty">Madurai Nayak dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thanjavur-nayak-kingdom">Thanjavur Nayak kingdom</a> weakened them, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gingee">Gingee</a> in 1649. The fort later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gingee-nayaks.jpg" alt="Gingee Nayaks" width="320" height="447"><figcaption>Image: Varungarde at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GingeeRajas_mandap.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1509 CE – 1649 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gingee</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Krishnappa Nayaka</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Telugu, Tamil</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nayaks of Senji, Senji Nayakas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1579&amp;focus=gingee-nayaks" aria-label="Gingee Nayaks on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6862 1721 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gingee Nayaks lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7182 1977L7088 1864M7182 1977L7060 1809"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7088 1864h0M7060 1809h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7182 1977h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7182 1977h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Gingee. <a href="/atlas?year=1579&amp;focus=gingee-nayaks">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

